# Aromachemical Failure Taxonomy
**Status:** PERMANENT RULEBOOK - Written in Stone
**Last Updated:** 2026-01-12
**Purpose:** Systematic classification of ALL failure modes for ALL aromachemicals

---

## Overview

This document classifies **every possible failure mode** by:
1. **Failure Type** (incompatibility, overdose, waste, degradation, toxicity)
2. **Chemical Class** (animalics, aldehydes, phenols, terpenes, musks, etc.)
3. **Intensity Tier** (ultra-potent, extremely potent, high impact, moderate, low)
4. **Base Compatibility** (clean, oriental, chypre, leather, fougère)

**Rule:** Every aromachemical falls into one or more categories. Apply ALL relevant rules.

---

## SECTION 1: FAILURE TYPE CLASSIFICATION

### 1.1 INCOMPATIBILITY FAILURES
**Definition:** Material chemically or perceptually incompatible with base type.

#### 1.1.1 Animalic-Clean Incompatibility (CRITICAL)
**Materials:** Skatole, indole (high conc.), oud, castoreum, civet, hyraceum, costus root
**Incompatible Bases:** Clean, transparent, aquatic, aldehydic, ozonic
**Compatible Bases:** Heavy oriental, leather, chypre, tobacco, gourmand (low conc.)
**Failure Mechanism:** 
- Animalic notes contain fecal/urine compounds (skatole = 3-methylindole)
- In clean bases, no masking → pure fecal odor dominates
- ODT extremely low (0.00002% skatole) → tiny overdose = disaster
**Maximum Safe Dose:**
- Clean base: 0% (NEVER use)
- Heavy oriental: 0.01-0.05% (pre-diluted to 1%)
- Leather base: 0.05-0.2% (pre-diluted to 1-10%)
**Historical Failures:** 
- Skatole 0.2 mL in BR540 100 mL = fecal disaster (OV = 20,000)
- Oud fleuressence 0.5 mL in BR540 = shit smell

#### 1.1.2 Aldehyde-Heavy Base Incompatibility
**Materials:** C-12 MNA, C-11 undecanol, C-14 peach, benzaldehyde, citral
**Incompatible Bases:** Heavy resinous (labdanum, benzoin, opoponax dominant)
**Compatible Bases:** Clean, soapy, floral, citrus, ozonic
**Failure Mechanism:**
- Aldehydes = sparkling, metallic, "lift"
- Heavy resins = thick, sticky, balsamic, "weight"
- Perceptual clash: aldehydes buried under resin, waste of material
**Maximum Safe Dose:**
- In resinous base: <0.5% (will be masked)
- In clean base: 1-5% (appropriate use)
**Prevention:** Check base composition for >10% resins before adding aldehydes

#### 1.1.3 Fresh Green-Oriental Incompatibility
**Materials:** Cis-3-hexenol, galbanum, violet leaf (undecavertol), green aldehydes
**Incompatible Bases:** Heavy sweet orientals (vanilla, tonka, benzoin dominant)
**Compatible Bases:** Chypre, fougère, cologne, aquatic
**Failure Mechanism:**
- Fresh green = sharp, grassy, cucumber-like
- Sweet oriental = warm, gourmand, enveloping
- Perceptual discord: "salad in a bakery"
**Maximum Safe Dose:**
- In oriental: <0.3% (accent only)
- In chypre/fougère: 2-8% (structural)

#### 1.1.4 Marine-Resinous Incompatibility
**Materials:** Calone, helional, sea breeze accords, ozonic notes
**Incompatible Bases:** Heavy amber, incense, myrrh, opoponax
**Compatible Bases:** Aquatic, cologne, citrus, light florals
**Failure Mechanism:**
- Marine = airy, watermelon, oceanic
- Resinous = dense, smoky, church-like
- Perceptual clash: "beach in a cathedral"

---

### 1.2 OVERDOSE FAILURES
**Definition:** Correct material, correct base, WRONG QUANTITY.

#### 1.2.1 Ultra-Potent Overdose (ODT <0.00001%)
**Materials:** β-damascenone, 2-methyl-3-furanthiol, grapefruit mercaptan, furaneol (high conc.)
**ODT Range:** 0.000001 - 0.00001%
**Safe Concentration Range:** 0.0001 - 0.001% in final fragrance
**OV Thresholds:**
- OV <100: Subtle, nuanced
- OV 100-500: Noticeable, pleasant
- OV 500-1,000: Strong, clear
- OV >1,000: Overpowering, unbalanced
- OV >5,000: Catastrophic, unwearable
**Dosage Formula:** 
```
Safe Dose (mL) = (ODT × Target OV × Base Volume) / 100
Target OV = 500 (recommended for ultra-potents)
```
**Example:** 
- β-damascenone in 100 mL base, target OV = 500
- Safe dose = (0.000001 × 500 × 100) / 100 = 0.0005 mL = 0.5 µL
- Use 1:1000 dilution, add 0.5 mL of dilution
**Historical Failures:** None yet, but inevitable if used neat

#### 1.2.2 Extremely Potent Overdose (ODT 0.00001-0.0001%)
**Materials:** Indole, skatole, methyl anthranilate, γ-decalactone, ionones
**ODT Range:** 0.00001 - 0.0001%
**Safe Concentration Range:** 0.001 - 0.05% (indole/skatole lower end)
**OV Thresholds:**
- OV <1,000: Safe, balanced
- OV 1,000-5,000: High, risky
- OV 5,000-10,000: Extreme, likely failure
- OV >10,000: Catastrophic (proven)
**Mandatory Pre-Dilution:**
- Skatole: 1% max (preferably 0.1%)
- Indole: 10% max (floral contexts only)
- Ionones: Neat OK if <1% final
**Historical Failures:**
- Skatole neat → OV 20,000+ = fecal

#### 1.2.3 High Impact Overdose (ODT 0.0001-0.01%)
**Materials:** Linalool, eugenol, citronellol, geraniol, phenethyl alcohol
**ODT Range:** 0.0015 - 0.01%
**Safe Concentration Range:** 0.5 - 8% (material-dependent)
**OV Thresholds:**
- OV <500: Subtle support
- OV 500-2,000: Balanced, clear character
- OV 2,000-5,000: Strong, may dominate
- OV >5,000: Overpowering
**Commercial Norms (from formula analysis):**
- Linalool: 1-8% (typical 3%)
- Geraniol: 1-5% (typical 2-3%)
- Eugenol: 0.5-3% (IFRA restricted)
**Overdose Detection:** If dose >2× commercial extreme, flag as abnormal

#### 1.2.4 Moderate Impact Overdose (ODT 0.01-0.1%)
**Materials:** Limonene, hedione, benzyl salicylate, coumarin
**ODT Range:** 0.02 - 0.1%
**Safe Concentration Range:** 1 - 15%
**OV Thresholds:**
- OV <200: Very subtle
- OV 200-1,000: Balanced
- OV 1,000-3,000: Strong, character-defining
- OV >3,000: Excessive
**Generic Booster Warning:**
- Hedione: Often added unnecessarily as "radiance booster"
  - **Rule:** Only add if base is flat/linear (lacks sparkle)
  - Max recommended: 2% (commercial norm: 0.5-2%)
  - Overdose: >3% = wasteful, metallic
- Benzyl salicylate: "Floral fixative" added without diagnosis
  - **Rule:** Only if florals fade too fast OR need balsamic depth
  - Max recommended: 8%

#### 1.2.5 Low Impact Overdose (ODT >0.1%)
**Materials:** Vanillin, ethyl vanillin, iso E super, galaxolide, ambroxan
**ODT Range:** 0.12 - 0.8%
**Safe Concentration Range:** 1 - 20% (iso E super up to 65% in outliers like Escentric 01)
**OV Thresholds:**
- OV <50: Barely noticeable
- OV 50-500: Balanced support
- OV 500-2,000: Strong, clear
- OV >2,000: Dominant (OK for musks/woods, not for vanillin)
**Overdose Scenarios:**
- Vanillin >10%: Cloying, candy-like, nauseating
- Iso E super >20%: Metallic, headache-inducing
- Galaxolide >15%: Detergent-like, synthetic

---

### 1.3 WASTE FAILURES
**Definition:** Expensive material used incorrectly (large batch, wrong context, better alternatives exist).

#### 1.3.1 Large Batch Waste
**Rule:** NEVER use expensive materials (>$0.50/mL) in large batches (>50 mL) without testing.
**Expensive Materials Database:**
| Material | Cost/mL | Owned Substitute | Substitute Ratio |
|----------|---------|------------------|------------------|
| Iris natural | $5.00 | alpha-ionone, alpha irone, irotyl, orris FTEC, alpha isomethyl ionone | 0.3 |
| Rose absolute | $10.00 | phenethyl alcohol, geranium FO, rose oxide 10% | 0.4 |
| Jasmine absolute | $8.00 | hedione, indole, jasmine FO | 0.5 |
| Oud absolute | $15.00 | Arabian oud fleuressence, AVOID in clean bases | 0.5 |
| Sandalwood oil | $3.00 | ebanol, bacdanol, sandalwood FO | 0.6 |
| Oakmoss absolute | $2.00 | evernyl | 0.5 |
| Saffron | $4.00 | saffranal, saffron FTEC | 0.3 |
| Benzoin Siam | $0.70 | vanillin, ethyl maltol, tonka bean FO | 0.5 |

**Historical Failures:**
- Benzoin 10 mL in 100 mL batch = $7 wasted (could have used vanillin 5 mL = $0.50)
**Prevention Algorithm:**
1. IF material in expensive list AND dose >5 mL AND batch >50 mL → ERROR
2. Check owned substitutes first
3. If owned substitute exists → recommend owned material
4. If no owned substitute → warn "test in 10-20 mL batch first"

#### 1.3.2 Context Waste
**Rule:** Don't use expensive materials where cheaper alternatives perform identically.
**Examples:**
- Natural rose absolute in body lotion (washed off) → use geraniol + phenethyl alcohol
- Orris butter in soap (goes down drain) → use alpha-ionone
- Real jasmine in room spray (diluted) → use hedione + indole
**Prevention:** Check product type and justify expensive material use

#### 1.3.3 Redundancy Waste
**Rule:** Don't use multiple materials with identical function/character.
**Examples:**
- Linalool + linalyl acetate + lavender EO (all contain linalool) → redundant, use ONE
- Vanillin + ethyl vanillin + tonka + benzoin (all sweet vanilla) → pick 1-2 max
- Ambroxan + ambrofix + cetalox + ISO E super (all woody-amber) → pick 1-2 max
**Prevention:** Analyze chemical composition and odor character before layering

---

### 1.4 DEGRADATION FAILURES
**Definition:** Chemically unstable materials that oxidize, polymerize, or decompose.

#### 1.4.1 Oxidation-Prone Materials
**Materials:** Citrus oils (limonene, citral), terpenes, aldehydes, phenols
**Failure Mechanism:**
- Oxidation by air (O₂) → off-notes (turpentine, rancid, metallic)
- UV light accelerates degradation
- Metal ions (Fe, Cu) catalyze oxidation
**Prevention:**
- Add BHT (butylated hydroxytoluene) 0.01-0.1% as antioxidant
- Store in amber glass, airtight
- Avoid copper/iron containers
- Use within 6 months of opening
**Materials Requiring BHT:**
- D-limonene (VP = 206 Pa, highly volatile, oxidizes to carvone/limonene oxide)
- Bergamot EO (limonene + linalool + linalyl acetate)
- Linalool (oxidizes to linalool oxides)
- Aldehydes (C-12 MNA → carboxylic acids)

#### 1.4.2 Hydrolysis-Prone Materials
**Materials:** Esters (linalyl acetate, benzyl acetate, terpinyl acetate), lactones
**Failure Mechanism:**
- Water (moisture) + acid/base → ester + alcohol breakdown
- Linalyl acetate → linalool + acetic acid (loses crisp character)
**Prevention:**
- Keep formulas anhydrous (<1% water)
- pH 4-7 (neutral, avoid extremes)
- Add antioxidants (BHT, vitamin E)

#### 1.4.3 Photodegradation Materials
**Materials:** Bergamot (bergapten/furanocoumarins), citrus oils, vanillin
**Failure Mechanism:**
- UV light → molecular breakdown → color change (browning)
- Bergapten → phototoxicity (skin burns under sunlight)
**Prevention:**
- Use bergamot FCF (furanocoumarin-free)
- Amber bottles
- UV filters in product
- IFRA limits: Bergapten <15 ppm in skin products

---

### 1.5 TOXICITY/REGULATORY FAILURES
**Definition:** Exceeds IFRA/safety limits, causes sensitization or irritation.

#### 1.5.1 IFRA-Restricted Materials
| Material | IFRA Category 4 Limit (Hydroalcoholic) | Reason |
|----------|--------------------------------------|--------|
| Coumarin | 0.6% | Hepatotoxicity (liver) |
| Eugenol | 1.0% | Sensitization |
| Isoeugenol | 0.2% | Sensitization |
| Geraniol | 5.4% | Sensitization (mild) |
| Citral | 2.6% | Sensitization |
| Hydroxycitronellal | 0.4% | Sensitization (listed allergen) |
| Bergamot | 0.4% bergapten | Phototoxicity |
| Oakmoss absolute | 0.1% | Sensitization (atranol) |

**Rule:** ALWAYS check IFRA limit before dosing. If dose >80% of limit, warn.

#### 1.5.2 Allergen Declaration
**Rule:** If total allergen content >0.001% (10 ppm), must declare on label.
**Common Allergens (EU 26):**
- Linalool, limonene, geraniol, citronellol, citral
- Eugenol, isoeugenol, benzyl benzoate, benzyl salicylate
- Coumarin, farnesol, hydroxycitronellal
**Prevention:** Track allergen total in validator

---

### 1.6 ACCORD DESTRUCTION FAILURES
**Definition:** Adding a material destroys an existing accord's character.

#### 1.6.1 Floral Accord Destruction
**Vulnerable Accords:** Rose, jasmine, lily of the valley, tuberose, violet
**Destructive Materials:**
| Material | Destruction Mechanism | Threshold |
|----------|----------------------|-----------|
| Musks (>10%) | Overwhelm delicate florals, become "laundry" | >10% galaxolide/ethylene brassylate |
| Animalics (>0.01%) | Fecal overpowers floral | >0.01% indole in non-jasmine |
| Patchouli (>3%) | Earthiness dominates, loses freshness | >3% in light florals |
| Woods (>8%) | Dries out, loses lushness | >8% cedarwood/sandalwood |
| Citrus (>15%) | Acidic clash with soft petals | >15% limonene |

**Prevention:**
- Check floral % in base before adding heavy materials
- If floral >20% of base → restrict musks to <8%, woods to <5%

#### 1.6.2 Citrus Accord Destruction
**Vulnerable Accords:** Bergamot, lemon, orange, grapefruit, neroli
**Destructive Materials:**
| Material | Destruction Mechanism | Threshold |
|----------|----------------------|-----------|
| Resins (>5%) | Sticky heaviness kills freshness | >5% benzoin/labdanum |
| Vanilla (>3%) | Sweet weight destroys crisp character | >3% vanillin |
| Animalics (any) | Ruins clean/fresh impression | >0.001% skatole |
| Heavy woods (>5%) | Loses sparkling top | >5% vetiver |

**Prevention:**
- Citrus accords need <5% base notes total in top-heavy formulas
- If citrus >30% of formula → restrict resins to <3%

#### 1.6.3 Amber/Oriental Accord Destruction
**Vulnerable Accords:** Amber, vanilla, benzoin, labdanum, tonka
**Destructive Materials:**
| Material | Destruction Mechanism | Threshold |
|----------|----------------------|-----------|
| Fresh greens (>1%) | Clashing temperature (cold vs warm) | >1% cis-3-hexenol |
| Marine notes (>0.5%) | "Beach in cathedral" discord | >0.5% calone |
| Aldehydes (>2%) | Metallic intrusion | >2% C-12 MNA |
| High citrus (>10%) | Acidic interference | >10% limonene |

**Prevention:**
- If amber/oriental >40% of base → restrict fresh notes to <1%
- Amber accords are WARM → cold notes destroy coherence

#### 1.6.4 Leather Accord Destruction
**Vulnerable Accords:** Birch tar, suede, castoreum-style, smoky leather
**Destructive Materials:**
| Material | Destruction Mechanism | Threshold |
|----------|----------------------|-----------|
| Fruity notes (>3%) | Inappropriate sweetness | >3% γ-decalactone |
| Sweet musks (>8%) | Loses edge, becomes soft | >8% galaxolide |
| Powdery iris (>5%) | Feminizes masculine leather | >5% alpha-ionone |
| Aldehydes (>3%) | Wrong texture (soapy vs. raw) | >3% C-12 |

**Prevention:**
- Leather needs EDGE → sweet/soft materials destroy character
- If leather >15% → restrict fruity/sweet to <2%

---

### 1.7 LAYER/PYRAMID FAILURES
**Definition:** Volatility mismatches cause pyramid collapse or note competition.

#### 1.7.1 Top Note Collapse (Flash-Off)
**Problem:** Top notes evaporate too fast, nothing left after 15 minutes
**Causes:**
| Cause | Example | Prevention |
|-------|---------|------------|
| Too much high-VP material | >40% limonene (VP=206 Pa) | Cap volatile citrus at 30% |
| No fixatives | All citrus/aldehyde, no musks | Add 5-10% low-VP fixative |
| High alcohol flash | EdP in spray, top notes flash off | Use 0.5-1% fixative in top |
| No bridge to heart | Gap between top and heart notes | Include mid-volatility materials |

**Volatile Materials (VP >1 Pa at 25°C):**
- Limonene (206 Pa) - EXTREMELY volatile
- Linalool (0.2 Pa) - volatile
- Linalyl acetate (0.1 Pa) - volatile
- Aldehydes (C-10 to C-12) - volatile

**Fixative Requirement:**
- If top notes >30% of formula → REQUIRE ≥5% fixative (musk, ambroxan, iso E super)
- If top notes >50% → formula will collapse, warn "short-lived fragrance"

#### 1.7.2 Heart Note Disappearance (Skip Effect)
**Problem:** Heart notes get overwhelmed, perception jumps from top to base
**Causes:**
| Cause | Example | Prevention |
|-------|---------|------------|
| Overpowered by base | 60% base, 10% heart | Balance to 30-40% heart |
| Base ODT too low | Skatole OV=10,000 vs. rose OV=500 | Calculate OV for all layers |
| Volatility gap | Huge VP difference top↔heart | Use bridging materials |
| Character clash | Heavy base swallows delicate heart | Match character/weight |

**Prevention:**
- Heart should be 25-40% of formula
- Heart materials should have moderate VP (0.001-0.1 Pa)
- Calculate OV for heart notes - if any base note OV >3× heart OV, base will dominate

#### 1.7.3 Base Note Dominance (Heavy Bottom)
**Problem:** Base notes overwhelm everything, formula smells one-dimensional
**Causes:**
| Cause | Example | Prevention |
|-------|---------|------------|
| Too much base | >60% musks + woods | Cap base at 50% |
| Ultra-low ODT base | Skatole, ambroxan at high % | Calculate OV for ALL materials |
| Multiple fixatives | Ambroxan + ISO E + galaxolide | Limit to 2-3 fixatives total |
| Missing top lift | All base, no sparkle | Add 10-20% top notes |

**Prevention:**
- Base notes should be 30-50% of formula (not 70%+)
- If base OV total >5000 → warn "will smell heavy/cloying"

#### 1.7.4 Note Competition (Same Layer Clash)
**Problem:** Two materials in same layer fight for dominance
**Examples:**
| Clash | Why | Solution |
|-------|-----|----------|
| Bergamot + Lemon | Both citrus tops, redundant | Use ONE or blend intentionally |
| Rose + Jasmine (equal) | Both floral hearts, compete | Let one dominate (2:1 ratio) |
| Sandalwood + Vetiver | Both woody bases, different character | Choose one as anchor |
| Vanilla + Tonka (equal) | Redundant sweetness | Use one, other as trace accent |

**Rule:** Within each layer, ONE material should dominate (50%+ of that layer)
**Prevention:**
- If two materials in same layer are within 20% of each other → warn "competition"
- Suggest making one dominant (2:1 or 3:1 ratio)

---

### 1.8 MASKING FAILURES
**Definition:** A powerful material masks other materials, wasting them.

#### 1.8.1 OV-Based Masking
**Rule:** If Material A has OV >3× Material B, Material B is likely masked
**Examples:**
| Masker | OV | Masked | OV | Result |
|--------|-----|--------|-----|--------|
| Skatole 0.1% | 5000 | Rose 2% | 400 | Rose invisible, skatole dominates |
| ISO E 15% | 30 | Linalool 3% | 2000 | Linalool dominates (reverse masking) |
| Ambroxan 5% | 166 | Hedione 2% | 40 | Hedione may be masked |

**Prevention:**
- Calculate OV for ALL materials
- Flag if any OV >3× another material in same layer
- Suggest rebalancing or removing lower-impact material

#### 1.8.2 Character-Based Masking
**Rule:** Heavy/dense characters mask light/airy characters
| Heavy (Masker) | Light (Masked) |
|----------------|----------------|
| Oud, labdanum, benzoin | Citrus, aldehyde, green |
| Patchouli, vetiver | Floral, fruity |
| Vanilla, tonka | Fresh, aquatic |
| Musks (high %) | Delicate florals |

**Prevention:**
- If heavy material >15% AND light material <5% → warn "light notes will be masked"
- Suggest increasing light material or reducing heavy

#### 1.8.3 Resinous Burial
**Rule:** Resins (labdanum, benzoin, myrrh) "bury" lighter materials
**Threshold:** If total resin >10%, expect masking of:
- Aldehydes (almost completely masked)
- Fresh greens (masked)
- Light florals (partially masked)
- Citrus (partially masked)

**Prevention:**
- If resin >10% → warn about masking
- If adding light material to resin-heavy base → "will be masked, use >3% or reconsider"

---

### 1.9 CARRIER/DILUTION FAILURES
**Definition:** Carrier solvent or dilution level affects material behavior.

#### 1.9.1 Carrier Incompatibility
| Carrier | Properties | Compatible Materials | Incompatible |
|---------|------------|---------------------|--------------|
| Ethanol | Polar, fast evaporation | Most materials | Some naturals precipitate |
| DPG | Non-volatile, extends | Dilutions, accords | May slow top notes |
| IPM (Isopropyl Myristate) | Oily, skin-feel | Base notes, musks | Aldehydes (can react) |
| Benzyl Benzoate | Fixative, mild odor | Florals, musks | May add own scent |
| Perfumer's Alcohol | Industry standard | All | None (ideal carrier) |

**Rules:**
- Pre-diluted materials in DPG → will behave differently than neat
- If material is "10% in DPG" → actual dose = stated dose × 0.1
- DPG extends longevity but may slow opening

#### 1.9.2 Dilution Factor Calculation
**CRITICAL:** Many materials are pre-diluted. Account for this!
| Form | Actual Concentration | Dose Adjustment |
|------|---------------------|-----------------|
| Neat | 100% | Use as stated |
| 10% in DPG | 10% | Multiply dose by 10 |
| 1% in DPG | 1% | Multiply dose by 100 |
| 1:1000 solution | 0.1% | Multiply dose by 1000 |

**Example:**
- Want 0.1% β-damascenone in 100 mL
- Material is 1:1000 solution (0.1%)
- Need: 0.1% × 100 mL = 0.1 mL actual damascenone
- From 0.1% solution: 0.1 mL ÷ 0.001 = 100 mL (entire bottle!)
- **WRONG** - recalculate for pre-diluted materials

**Prevention:**
- ALWAYS check material form (neat vs. diluted)
- If material in inventory says "10% in DPG" → adjust calculations

#### 1.9.3 Concentration-Dependent Character Changes
**Rule:** Some materials smell DIFFERENT at different concentrations
| Material | Low Conc Character | High Conc Character | Threshold |
|----------|-------------------|---------------------|-----------|
| Indole | Floral, jasmine | Fecal, mothballs | 0.1% |
| Skatole | Animalic-warm | Pure feces | 0.01% |
| Linalool | Fresh, lavender | Harsh, chemical | 8% |
| ISO E Super | Woody, velvet | Metallic, headache | 15% |
| Coumarin | Hay, tonka | Bitter, medicinal | 3% |
| Eugenol | Warm, spicy | Dentist office | 2% |

**Prevention:**
- Track concentration-dependent character changes
- Warn if dose crosses character threshold

---

### 1.10 SYNERGY/ANTAGONISM FAILURES
**Definition:** Materials interact unexpectedly (positively or negatively).

#### 1.10.1 Known Antagonisms (Bad Combinations)
| Material A | Material B | Result | Avoid |
|------------|-----------|--------|-------|
| Aldehydes | Phenols | Discoloration over time | C-12 + eugenol |
| Citrus | Animalics | Perceptual clash | Lemon + skatole |
| Marine | Resins | "Beach in cathedral" | Calone + labdanum |
| Vanilla | Fresh greens | Temperature clash | Vanillin + cis-3-hexenol |
| Musks (clean) | Oud | Incompatible characters | Galaxolide + oud |

**Prevention:**
- Check formula for known antagonistic pairs
- If found → warn "may clash, test small batch"

#### 1.10.2 Known Synergies (Good Combinations)
| Material A | Material B | Result | Recommended Ratio |
|------------|-----------|--------|-------------------|
| Vanilla | Tonka | Enhanced warmth | 2:1 vanilla:tonka |
| Sandalwood | Iris | Creamy powder | 1:1 to 2:1 |
| Bergamot | Hedione | Radiant freshness | 5:1 bergamot:hedione |
| Oakmoss | Patchouli | Classic chypre | 1:2 |
| Amber | Labdanum | Deep warmth | 1:1 |
| Jasmine | Indole (trace) | Natural jasmine effect | Jasmine + 0.01% indole |

**Opportunity:**
- If user adds one synergy partner → suggest the other
- "You're using sandalwood - consider adding iris for creamy powder effect"

#### 1.10.3 Chemical Reactions (Degradation)
| Reaction | Materials | Result | Prevention |
|----------|-----------|--------|------------|
| Oxidation | Terpenes + O₂ | Turpentine, rancid | Add BHT |
| Aldol condensation | Aldehydes + Aldehydes | Color change | Limit aldehyde mixing |
| Esterification | Acids + Alcohols | Character change | Control pH |
| Schiff base | Aldehydes + Amines | New compounds | Avoid mixing |

**Prevention:**
- If formula contains reactive classes → warn about stability
- Recommend accelerated aging test (heat, light) before large batch

---

### 1.11 AGING/MATURATION FAILURES
**Definition:** Formula changes over time in unexpected ways.

#### 1.11.1 Poor Agers (Materials that degrade)
| Material | Aging Behavior | Timeline | Prevention |
|----------|----------------|----------|------------|
| Citrus (limonene) | Oxidizes → turpentine | 3-6 months | BHT, dark storage |
| Aldehydes | Oxidizes → acids | 6-12 months | BHT, airtight |
| Linalool | Oxidizes → off-notes | 6 months | BHT |
| Jasmine absolute | May develop indolic | 1+ years | Normal, not failure |

#### 1.11.2 Good Agers (Materials that improve)
| Material | Aging Behavior | Timeline |
|----------|----------------|----------|
| Resins (benzoin, labdanum) | Smoother, richer | 1-3 months |
| Sandalwood | Creamier | 3+ months |
| Oakmoss | Integrates | 1-2 months |
| Musks | Blend better | 2-4 weeks |

#### 1.11.3 Maceration Time Requirements
| Formula Type | Minimum Maceration | Ideal |
|--------------|-------------------|-------|
| Simple (3-5 materials) | 1 week | 2-4 weeks |
| Moderate (6-15 materials) | 2 weeks | 4-8 weeks |
| Complex (16+ materials) | 4 weeks | 8-12 weeks |
| Contains resins/naturals | 4 weeks minimum | 8+ weeks |

**Prevention:**
- Warn if user tests immediately after mixing
- "Formula contains resins - macerate 4+ weeks before final judgment"

---

### 1.12 BATCH SIZE FAILURES
**Definition:** What works in small batch fails in large batch (or vice versa).

#### 1.12.1 Scale-Up Issues
| Issue | Cause | Example | Prevention |
|-------|-------|---------|------------|
| Precipitation | Solubility limits | Musk crystals at scale | Pre-dissolve, heat gently |
| Separation | Oil/water separation | Natural oils in alcohol | Add co-solvent (DPG) |
| Uneven mixing | Poor homogenization | Large batch streaking | Mix thoroughly, let settle |
| Heat generation | Exothermic mixing | Large alcohol additions | Add slowly, temperature control |

#### 1.12.2 Small Batch Limitations
| Issue | Cause | Prevention |
|-------|-------|------------|
| Measurement error | Sub-0.1 mL doses impossible | Use dilutions |
| Evaporation loss | More surface area ratio | Seal immediately |
| Oxidation speed | More air exposure | Fill container completely |

**Rule:** Test in 10-20 mL batch first, then scale up
**Exception:** Ultra-potent materials → test in 5 mL batch

---

## SECTION 2: CHEMICAL CLASS RULES

### 2.1 ANIMALICS (Fecal/Urine/Musk Character)
**Members:** Skatole, indole (>1%), civet, castoreum, costus, hyraceum, cumin (high dose)
**Chemical Properties:**
- Heterocyclic nitrogen compounds (indole, pyrroles) or sulfur compounds
- Extremely low ODT (0.00001-0.0001%)
- Fecal character at high conc., floral at ultra-low
**Base Compatibility Matrix:**
| Base Type | Compatibility | Max Dose | Notes |
|-----------|---------------|----------|-------|
| Clean/Transparent | ❌ NEVER | 0% | Pure fecal, no masking |
| Aquatic/Marine | ❌ NEVER | 0% | Clash, ruins fresh character |
| Aldehydic/Soapy | ❌ NEVER | 0% | Fecal dominates clean |
| Light Floral | ⚠️ RISKY | 0.001-0.01% indole only | Jasmine context, pre-diluted 10% |
| Heavy Oriental | ✓ OK | 0.01-0.1% | Masked by vanilla/resin |
| Leather | ✓ GOOD | 0.05-0.3% | Synergistic, birch tar support |
| Chypre (Mossy) | ✓ OK | 0.02-0.15% | Oakmoss/patchouli mask |
| Tobacco | ✓ GOOD | 0.05-0.2% | Enhances depth |
| Gourmand | ⚠️ RISKY | 0.01-0.05% | Only if heavy (chocolate/coffee) |

**MANDATORY RULES:**
1. NEVER use in clean, aquatic, aldehydic, or ozonic bases
2. ALWAYS pre-dilute to 0.1-10% before adding
3. ALWAYS calculate OV, reject if >1,000 for skatole, >5,000 for indole
4. TEST in 5 mL batch first, never large batch directly
5. If user wants animalic in clean base → suggest clean musks (exaltolide, galaxolide, ambroxan)

---

### 2.2 ALDEHYDES (Sparkling/Metallic/Soapy)
**Members:** C-12 MNA, C-11 undecanol, C-14 peach, benzaldehyde, citral, helional
**Chemical Properties:**
- Carbonyl group (C=O) → reactive, oxidizes easily
- Volatile (high VP) → top note
- Metallic, sparkling, "clean laundry" character
**Base Compatibility Matrix:**
| Base Type | Compatibility | Max Dose | Notes |
|-----------|---------------|----------|-------|
| Clean/Transparent | ✓ EXCELLENT | 1-5% | Signature ingredient |
| Aldehydic/Soapy | ✓ EXCELLENT | 3-10% | Define category |
| Floral Bouquet | ✓ GOOD | 1-4% | Lift, sparkle, Chanel No. 5 style |
| Citrus/Cologne | ✓ GOOD | 0.5-3% | Accent, metallic edge |
| Heavy Oriental | ❌ POOR | <0.5% | Buried under resins, wasted |
| Resinous/Amber | ❌ POOR | <0.3% | Incompatible weight |
| Leather | ⚠️ OK | 0.5-2% | Suede/birch context only |

**MANDATORY RULES:**
1. Check base for resin content (labdanum, benzoin, myrrh, opoponax)
2. If resin >10% → warn "aldehydes will be masked, use <0.5%"
3. Add BHT 0.05% to prevent oxidation (aldehydes → acids)
4. C-12 MNA: EXTREMELY POWERFUL, use 0.1-1% max

---

### 2.3 PHENOLS (Spicy/Medicinal/Clove-like)
**Members:** Eugenol, isoeugenol, thymol, carvacrol, guaiacol
**Chemical Properties:**
- Aromatic ring with -OH group
- Reactive with aldehydes (can cause discoloration)
- IFRA restricted (sensitization)
**Base Compatibility Matrix:**
| Base Type | Compatibility | Max Dose | Notes |
|-----------|---------------|----------|-------|
| Spice/Oriental | ✓ EXCELLENT | 0.5-3% | Clove, cinnamon character |
| Carnation/Floral | ✓ GOOD | 0.3-2% | Eugenol = carnation |
| Fougère | ✓ OK | 0.2-1% | Spicy accent |
| Leather | ✓ OK | 0.3-1.5% | Smoky depth |
| Clean/Aquatic | ❌ POOR | <0.2% | Medicinal clash |
| Aldehydic | ⚠️ RISKY | <0.5% | May react, discolor |

**MANDATORY RULES:**
1. ALWAYS check IFRA limit (eugenol 1.0%, isoeugenol 0.2%)
2. If formula contains aldehydes → warn "phenols + aldehydes may discolor"
3. Track allergen total

---

### 2.4 TERPENES (Fresh/Citrus/Pine/Green)
**Members:** Limonene, pinene, myrcene, ocimene, terpinolene
**Chemical Properties:**
- Hydrocarbon (C₁₀H₁₆) → volatile, oxidizes easily
- High VP (>100 Pa) → evaporates fast
- Fresh, sharp, natural character
**Degradation Risk:** HIGH (oxidation → off-notes)
**Prevention:**
- Add BHT 0.01-0.1%
- Amber glass storage
- Use within 6 months
**Base Compatibility:** Universal (citrus, green, woody, floral), but fades fast

**MANDATORY RULES:**
1. IF terpene >10% in formula → REQUIRE BHT addition
2. Warn about oxidation risk, short shelf life
3. Limonene ODT = 0.02% → calculate OV if >5%

---

### 2.5 MUSKS (Soft/Clean/Laundry/Animalic)
**Members:** Galaxolide, exaltolide, habanolide, cashmeran, ambrettolide
**Chemical Properties:**
- Macrocyclic (large ring) or polycyclic structures
- Low VP (<0.001 Pa) → fixative, long-lasting
- Soft, enveloping, skin-like
**Subtypes:**
- **Clean musks:** Galaxolide, ethylene brassylate → laundry, fresh
- **Animalic musks:** Exaltolide, ambrettolide → powdery, skin
- **Woody musks:** Cashmeran, habanolide → soft woods
**Base Compatibility:** Nearly universal
**Overdose Risk:** Moderate (can become cloying, detergent-like if >15%)

**MANDATORY RULES:**
1. If clean musk >12% → warn "may smell like laundry detergent"
2. Good substitute for animalics in clean bases (exaltolide instead of civet)

---

### 2.6 IONONES/IRONES (Violet/Iris/Woody/Powdery)
**Members:** Alpha-ionone, beta-ionone, alpha irone, gamma irone, methyl ionone
**Chemical Properties:**
- C₁₃ ketones with violet/iris character
- Expensive (irones) vs. affordable (ionones)
- Powdery, lipstick-like, nostalgic
**Cost Tiers:**
- Alpha-ionone: $0.30/mL (owned)
- Beta-ionone: $0.40/mL (owned)
- Alpha irone: $3-5/mL (owned, 3% in carrier)
- Natural iris: $5-10/mL (NOT owned)
**Base Compatibility:** Universal, especially floral, powdery, oriental

**MANDATORY RULES:**
1. IF user wants iris → ALWAYS suggest owned substitutes first:
   - Alpha-ionone, alpha irone, irotyl, orris FTEC, alpha isomethyl ionone
2. Dose: 0.5-8% (ionones), 0.3-3% (irones, often pre-diluted)

---

### 2.7 LACTONES (Creamy/Coconut/Peach/Milky)
**Members:** Gamma-decalactone, delta-decalactone, massoia lactone, whiskey lactone
**Chemical Properties:**
- Cyclic esters with creamy, milky, fruity character
- Hydrolysis risk (water → opens ring)
**Base Compatibility:**
| Base Type | Compatibility | Max Dose |
|-----------|---------------|----------|
| Gourmand/Creamy | ✓ EXCELLENT | 0.5-5% |
| Fruity/Tropical | ✓ EXCELLENT | 0.5-3% |
| Floral (Tuberose, Jasmine) | ✓ GOOD | 0.2-2% |
| Clean/Aquatic | ⚠️ OK | 0.1-0.8% |
| Woody/Dry | ❌ POOR | <0.3% (clash) |

**MANDATORY RULES:**
1. Keep anhydrous (<1% water) to prevent hydrolysis
2. Gamma-decalactone (coconut) + vanilla = cliché gourmand, warn if overused

---

## SECTION 3: INTENSITY TIER RULES

### Tier 1: ULTRA-POTENT (ODT <0.00001%)
**Members:** β-damascenone, 2-methyl-3-furanthiol, grapefruit mercaptan
**Mandatory Actions:**
1. REJECT if dose will create OV >500
2. REQUIRE 1:1000 dilution (or pre-diluted)
3. NEVER use neat
4. Calculate exact µL dose, use precision syringe/pipette
**Example:**
- β-damascenone neat in 100 mL → 0.0005 mL (0.5 µL) for OV 500
- Impossible to measure → REQUIRE 1:1000 dilution, add 0.5 mL of dilution

### Tier 2: EXTREMELY POTENT (ODT 0.00001-0.0001%)
**Members:** Skatole, indole, ionones, methyl anthranilate
**Mandatory Actions:**
1. REJECT if OV >1,000 (skatole/indole), >3,000 (ionones)
2. REQUIRE pre-dilution if ODT <0.00005%
3. WARN if dose in large batch (test small first)
**Example:**
- Skatole 0.00002%, target OV 500 in 100 mL → dose = 0.01 mL (10 µL)
- Pre-dilute to 1%, add 1 mL of 1% solution

### Tier 3: HIGH IMPACT (ODT 0.0001-0.01%)
**Members:** Linalool, geraniol, eugenol, citronellol
**Mandatory Actions:**
1. Calculate OV, warn if >3,000
2. Compare to commercial usage, flag if >2× extreme
3. Check IFRA limits (eugenol, citronellol)
**Example:**
- Linalool 5% in EdP (typical), OV = 5/0.0015 = 3,333 (strong but OK)
- Linalool 20% = OV 13,333 → REJECT, abnormal

### Tier 4: MODERATE IMPACT (ODT 0.01-0.1%)
**Members:** Hedione, limonene, coumarin, benzyl salicylate
**Mandatory Actions:**
1. Check if material is actually needed (hedione, benzyl benzoate "booster" check)
2. Calculate OV if dose >5%
3. Compare to commercial norms
**Example:**
- Hedione 4% → OV = 4/0.05 = 80 (fine)
- But if base is already radiant → warn "unnecessary, hedione best for flat bases"

### Tier 5: LOW IMPACT (ODT >0.1%)
**Members:** Vanillin, iso E super, galaxolide, ambroxan
**Mandatory Actions:**
1. Warn if dose >10% (vanillin), >20% (iso E super, musks)
2. Check for cloying/metallic risk
**Example:**
- Vanillin 12% → warn "may be cloying, nausea risk"
- Iso E super 25% → warn "metallic, headache risk, commercial extreme is 15%"

---

## SECTION 4: BASE TYPE COMPATIBILITY MATRIX

### Clean/Transparent Bases
**Definition:** Aquatic, ozonic, aldehydic, soapy, fresh laundry, "shower gel" style
**Signature Materials:** Aldehydes (C-12), calone, helional, hedione, clean musks (galaxolide)
**FORBIDDEN Materials:** 
- Animalics (skatole, civet, oud) → fecal disaster
- Heavy resins (>3% labdanum/benzoin) → muddy, lose transparency
- Earthy notes (vetiver >2%, patchouli >1%) → too grounded
**Recommended Structure:**
- Top: Citrus, aldehydes, aquatic (30-40%)
- Heart: Hedione, clean florals, ionones (30-40%)
- Base: Clean musks, ambroxan, light woods (30-40%)

### Heavy Oriental Bases
**Definition:** Vanilla, amber, incense, spices, resins, gourmand
**Signature Materials:** Vanillin, benzoin, labdanum, tonka, cinnamon, oud (acceptable here)
**FORBIDDEN Materials:**
- Aldehydes (>1%) → wasted, buried
- Fresh greens → "salad in bakery" clash
- Marine → perceptual discord
**Recommended Structure:**
- Top: Spices, citrus (accent only, 10-20%)
- Heart: Florals, woods (20-30%)
- Base: Resins, vanilla, musks, amber (50-70%)

### Chypre Bases
**Definition:** Oakmoss, patchouli, bergamot, labdanum, dry woods
**Signature Materials:** Oakmoss/evernyl, patchouli, bergamot, cistus, vetiver
**FORBIDDEN Materials:**
- Sweet gourmands (>2% vanilla) → loses dry, mossy character
- Clean musks (>5%) → too soapy, not earthy enough
**Recommended Structure:**
- Top: Bergamot, citrus, aldehydes (20-30%)
- Heart: Rose, jasmine, florals (20-30%)
- Base: Oakmoss, patchouli, labdanum (40-60%)

### Leather Bases
**Definition:** Birch tar, castoreum, labdanum, tobacco, smoky, animalic OK
**Signature Materials:** Birch tar, suederal, saffron, labdanum, styrax, oud (good here)
**ALLOWED Materials:** Animalics (skatole 0.05-0.2%, indole, castoreum)
**Recommended Structure:**
- Top: Saffron, aldehydes (accent, 10-20%)
- Heart: Florals, spices (20-30%)
- Base: Birch, labdanum, animalics, woods (50-70%)

### Fougère Bases
**Definition:** Lavender, coumarin, oakmoss, herbaceous, masculine "barbershop"
**Signature Materials:** Lavender, coumarin, tonka, oakmoss, geranium
**Recommended Structure:**
- Top: Lavender, bergamot, citrus (30-40%)
- Heart: Geranium, florals, spices (20-30%)
- Base: Coumarin, oakmoss, woods (40-50%)

---

## SECTION 4.5: ACCORD-SPECIFIC FAILURE RULES

### Classic Accords and Their Vulnerabilities

#### 4.5.1 BR540 / Clean Saffron-Amber Accord
**Core Materials:** Saffron, hedione, ambroxan, cedarwood, ISO E super, jasmine
**Character:** Clean, transparent, "skin scent", warm but airy
**FORBIDDEN ADDITIONS:**
| Material | Why | Result if Added |
|----------|-----|-----------------|
| Animalics (any) | Fecal in transparency | Shit smell |
| Heavy oud | Too dark, incompatible | Muddy, fecal |
| Dense resins (>3%) | Loses transparency | Heavy, cloying |
| Patchouli (>2%) | Too earthy | Loses clean character |
| Indole (>0.01%) | Fecal at threshold | Jasmine becomes shit |

**Safe Additions:**
- More ambroxan (up to 10%)
- More hedione (up to 3%)
- Light musks (galaxolide, ethylene brassylate)
- Sandalwood replacers (ebanol, bacdanol)

#### 4.5.2 Rose Accord
**Core Materials:** Phenethyl alcohol, geraniol, citronellol, rose oxide, damascones
**Character:** Fresh, dewy, feminine, sometimes honeyed
**VULNERABLE TO:**
| Material | Threshold | Effect |
|----------|-----------|--------|
| Musks (clean) | >8% | Becomes "soapy rose" |
| Patchouli | >2% | Loses freshness |
| Indole | >0.05% | Becomes jasmine-like |
| Heavy woods | >5% | Dries out rose |
| Citrus | >10% | Acidic clash |

**Safe Additions:**
- Linalool (1-3%) - enhances freshness
- Geranium (1-2%) - natural synergy
- Violet leaf (trace) - green accent
- Light amber (<2%) - warmth

#### 4.5.3 Oud Accord
**Core Materials:** Oud (natural or synthetic), cypriol, guaiac wood, birch tar
**Character:** Smoky, animalic, medicinal, woody, dark
**VULNERABLE TO:**
| Material | Threshold | Effect |
|----------|-----------|--------|
| Clean musks | >5% | Character clash |
| Aldehydes | >1% | Metallic clash |
| Fresh greens | >0.5% | Temperature clash |
| Marine | Any | Total destruction |
| Light florals | >3% | Confusing mix |

**Good Additions:**
- Rose (classic oud-rose) - tested combo
- Saffron - Middle Eastern classic
- Incense/labdanum - synergy
- Leather notes - compatible darkness

#### 4.5.4 Vanilla/Gourmand Accord
**Core Materials:** Vanillin, ethyl vanillin, tonka, benzoin, ethyl maltol
**Character:** Sweet, warm, edible, comforting
**VULNERABLE TO:**
| Material | Threshold | Effect |
|----------|-----------|--------|
| Fresh greens | >0.5% | "Salad in bakery" |
| Marine/aquatic | Any | Total clash |
| Aldehydes | >1% | Metallic intrusion |
| Citrus (sour) | >5% | Acidic bite |
| Oakmoss | >1% | Mossy bitterness |

**Good Additions:**
- Spices (cinnamon, cardamom)
- Fruity notes (peach, coconut)
- Coffee, chocolate accords
- Amber, labdanum

#### 4.5.5 Citrus/Cologne Accord
**Core Materials:** Bergamot, lemon, orange, neroli, petitgrain
**Character:** Fresh, sparkling, uplifting, short-lived
**VULNERABLE TO:**
| Material | Threshold | Effect |
|----------|-----------|--------|
| Resins | >3% | Kills freshness |
| Vanilla | >2% | Loses crisp character |
| Animalics | Any | Fecal clash |
| Heavy woods | >5% | Weighs down |

**REQUIRES:**
- Fixatives (musks, ambroxan) to extend longevity
- BHT for stability (citrus oxidizes)

**Good Additions:**
- Hedione (radiance)
- Clean musks (longevity)
- Light florals (neroli)
- Green notes (acceptable)

#### 4.5.6 Leather Accord
**Core Materials:** Birch tar, suederal, castoreum replacers, labdanum, saffron
**Character:** Smoky, animalic, masculine, raw
**VULNERABLE TO:**
| Material | Threshold | Effect |
|----------|-----------|--------|
| Fruity (sweet) | >2% | Inappropriate sweetness |
| Clean musks | >6% | Loses edge |
| Powdery iris | >3% | Feminizes |
| Fresh aquatic | Any | Character destruction |

**Good Additions:**
- Oud (synergy)
- Tobacco (complementary)
- Incense (depth)
- Dark florals (rose, violet)

#### 4.5.7 Chypre Accord
**Core Materials:** Bergamot, oakmoss, patchouli, labdanum, rose/jasmine
**Character:** Bittersweet, mossy, sophisticated, green-earthy
**VULNERABLE TO:**
| Material | Threshold | Effect |
|----------|-----------|--------|
| Vanilla | >2% | Loses chypre dryness |
| Sweet musks | >5% | Softens too much |
| Marine | Any | Total clash |
| Heavy animalics | >0.1% | Overpowers moss |

**Classic Ratios:**
- Bergamot: 10-20%
- Oakmoss/Evernyl: 2-8%
- Patchouli: 3-10%
- Labdanum: 2-8%
- Florals: 5-15%

